.TH DARPA-SPILL-QUERY 1 "September 2026" "darpa_spillserver 1.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
darpa-spill-query \- query NOvA accelerator event timestamps from the command line
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B darpa-spill-query
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B darpa-spill-query
answers the same questions as the server's
.I /api/events
endpoint without a browser, reading either a local archive file or a running
.BR darpa-spill-server (1).
.PP
Results print as an aligned table by default, or as CSV or JSON for piping and
saving.
.SS Quoting hex signals
Accelerator signals are conventionally written with a leading dollar sign,
.BR $74 ", " $8F .
A shell expands an unquoted
.B $74
to nothing, so quote it:
.BR "\-\-signal '$74'" .
The spellings
.BR 74 ", " 0x74
and the registry names
.RB ( numi ", " one-hertz )
work unquoted.
.SH OPTIONS
.SS General
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show version information and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-signals
List every accelerator signal this tool accepts, with its carrier, decoded
type and meaning, then exit.
.SS Data source
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-config " " \fIFILE\fR
Read the archive path from this configuration file.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-database " " \fIFILE\fR
Read this SQLite archive directly.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-server " " \fIURL\fR
Query a running server instead of a local file, e.g.
.BR http://localhost:8080 .
.PP
With none of these, the archive path comes from the configuration file found by
the usual search.
.SS Selection
.TP
.BI \-\-start " TIME"
Start of the range, inclusive.
.TP
.BI \-\-end " TIME"
End of the range, exclusive. A date-only value covers that whole day.
.TP
.BI \-\-signal " SIG"
Accelerator signal such as
.BR '$74' .
May be repeated; repeats form a union.
.TP
.BI \-\-type " TYPE"
Decoded spill type, by name
.RB ( NUMI ", " BNB_TCLK )
or number. May be repeated.
.TP
.B \-\-last
Show only the most recent matching event.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " N"
Maximum rows to return.
.TP
.B \-\-desc
Newest first.
.TP
.BI \-\-timezone " ZONE"
Zone for times that carry none. Default \fBUTC\fR.
.SS Output
.TP
.BR \-f ", " \-\-format " " \fIFORMAT\fR
One of \fBtable\fR (default), \fBcsv\fR, \fBjson\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-columns " LIST"
Comma-separated subset of columns.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Write to \fIFILE\fR instead of standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-header
Omit the CSV header row.
.SH TIME EXPRESSIONS
Anywhere a
.I TIME
is accepted:
.TP
.B now
The current instant.
.TP
.BR today ", " yesterday
Midnight starting that day.
.TP
.BR \-2h ", " \-30m ", " \-7d ", " \-1w
Relative to now.
.TP
.BR 09:15 ", " 11:34:22
That clock time today.
.TP
.BR 2026\-07\-01 ", " "Jul 1, 2026" ", " 01\-Jul\-2026
A calendar date.
.TP
.B 2026\-07\-01T09:15:00Z
ISO 8601. An explicit offset always overrides \fB\-\-timezone\fR.
.TP
.BI nova: TICKS
NOvA base time, in 64 MHz ticks.
.TP
.BI unix: SECONDS
UNIX time.
.TP
.BI gps: SECONDS
.TQ
.IB gps: WEEK : TOW
GPS time.
.PP
Ranges are half-open,
.IR "start <= t < end" ,
so adjacent queries tile without reporting an event twice.
.SH OUTPUT COLUMNS
.BR nova_time ", " utc ", " utc_string ", " unix_sec ", " unix_nsec ", "
.BR gps ", " gps_seconds ", " gps_nsec ", " gps_psec ", " gps_week ", "
.BR gps_tow ", " gps_tow_exact ", " spill_type ", " spill_type_name ", "
.BR signal ", " signal_name ", " event_number ", " delta ", " pps_offset ", "
.BR source .
.PP
Prefer
.B gps
over
.BR gps_seconds / gps_nsec :
a NOvA tick is 15.625 ns, so a nanosecond field cannot represent one exactly
and truncates 625 ps of every tick.
.B gps
is good to the picosecond, which is exact, and its fractional part matches the
one the DAQ prints in
.BR utc_string .
.PP
An empty
.B signal
means the hardware preserved only the decoded spill type. That mapping is
many-to-one \(em
.B $1D
and
.B $1F
both decode to
.B BNB_TCLK
\(em so the exact signal is unrecoverable and is left blank rather than
guessed.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
The server could not be reached, or returned an error.
.TP
.B 2
A malformed argument: an unknown signal, column, timezone, or time.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Every
.B $74
NuMI spill between 1 July 2026 and today:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-query \-\-signal '$74' \-\-start 2026\-07\-01 \-\-end today
.EE
.RE
.PP
The 1 Hz events between 09:15 and 11:34 today, saved as CSV:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-query \-\-signal '$8f' \-\-start 09:15 \-\-end 11:34 \\
                   \-\-format csv \-o spills.csv
.EE
.RE
.PP
The most recent event, from a running server:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-query \-\-server http://localhost:8080 \-\-last
.EE
.RE
.PP
Just the timestamps, for a pipeline:
.PP
.RS
.EX
darpa\-spill\-query \-\-signal '$8f' \-\-start \-1h \\
                   \-\-format csv \-\-columns gps_seconds \-\-no\-header
.EE
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR darpa-spill-server (1),
.BR darpa_spillserver (3),
.BR nova-time-convert (1)
